# Modules, Crates, Collections & Iterators

## Organization ladder

```text
workspace → crate (Cargo.toml) → module tree (mod.rs / inline mod)
```

```rust
mod storage {
    pub struct Store;
    pub(crate) fn internal() {}
}
use storage::Store;
```

`pub` exposes; `pub(crate)` limits to the crate. Privacy by default keeps API
surfaces honest.

## The two workhorses

```rust
let mut todos: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
todos.push("ship".into());
let first = todos[0].clone();          // panics if empty!
let safe = todos.first();              // Option<&String>

use std::collections::HashMap;
let mut counts: HashMap<String, u32> = HashMap::new();
*counts.entry("rust".to_string()).or_insert(0) += 1;
```

Indexing asserts presence; `.get()`/`.first()` return Options. Choose HashMap
for key→value lookups, Vec for order.

## Iterators: declarative and lazy

```rust
let names = vec!["ada", "grace", "linus"];
let upper: Vec<String> = names.iter()
    .filter(|n| n.len() < 5)
    .map(|n| n.to_uppercase())
    .collect();

let sum: i32 = (1..=100).sum();        // ranges are iterators too
```

Adapters compose into pipelines; nothing runs until a consumer (`collect`,
`sum`, `for`) pulls. This is the pattern this very app uses to build its
catalog from embedded files.

examples.code:
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() {
    let words = ["alpha", "beta", "alpha", "gamma", "beta", "alpha"];
    let mut counts: HashMap<&str, u32> = HashMap::new();
    for w in words {
        *counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;
    }
    let mut ranked: Vec<(&str, u32)> = counts.into_iter().collect();
    ranked.sort_by_key(|(_, c)| std::cmp::Reverse(*c));
    for (word, count) in &ranked {
        println!("{word}: {count}");
    }
}

